le on peripheral blood (circulating blood), indicating compatibility with common clinical sampling and enabling evaluation of cells present in the bloodstream. [Data: Entities (1809, 1805); Relationships (3010)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"B-ALL is explicitly tied to B-cell lineage origin and subtype classification, which is consistent with immunophenotype-based characterization\",\n      \"explanation\": \"B-cell ALL (B-ALL) is stated to start in B-cell lymphocytes (B cells) and is described as a main subtype of ALL, with further subtyping possible based on immunophenotype (and genetic/chromosomal abnormalities). This aligns the disease entity with the community’s broader emphasis on immunophenotypic classification. [Data: Entities (2608, 3033, 1286); Relationships (4779, 5968)]\\n\\nSeparately, the \\\"B cell\\\" concept is described as relevant to leukemia classification and to immunophenotyping contexts where B-cell populations are characterized via cell-surface markers (e.g., CD20). This reinforces the role of surface-marker panels in lineage and subtype determination within B-lineage malignancies. [Data: Entities (2603, 5617, 5633); Relationships (12640, 12607)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Burkitt lymphoma and Burkitt leukemia (mature B-cell ALL) are described as essentially the same disease entity but managed differently from typical B-ALL\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Burkitt lymphoma is described as an aggressive mature B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma, and mature B-cell ALL (Burkitt leukemia) is described as essentially the same disease entity as Burkitt lymphoma. This establishes a close diagnostic/biological equivalence across lymphoma and leukemia presentations. [Data: Entities (2578, 2610, 2577); Relationships (4783, 4745)]\\n\\nDespite this equivalence, the data notes that Burkitt lymphoma/Burkitt leukemia may be treated differently than typical B-ALL, highlighting that correct classification has management implications beyond simple l